m static analysis to enforce logging is known practice. Might be common mundane. But specific to Annex III 1(a) data flows and paragraph-3 event types is specific. Might be 2 interesting. Let's pick 2? The description: \"unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\" vs \"interesting\". I'd say it's interesting but not unusual imaginative. Maybe 2.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 common strategies that violate no norms\n3 somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 radically violates...\n\nCI gate requiring instrumentation is acceptable. Common. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 significant change, or expensive/difficult\n1 totally infeasible\n\nWrite lightweight AST scanner into existing lint suite and wire into merge-request pipeline. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Maybe 3. Could be easy? Lightweight scanner maybe requires dev effort. I'd say 3.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 unreasonable unlikely to solve\n1 solves unrelated problem\n\nWill it solve logging mandate? It ensures instrumentation present at merge, catches gaps early. That addresses compliance. Seems will solve main problem of meeting logging mandate for prototypes. Could be partial because doesn't guarantee runtime logs correct? But effectiveness rating ignores workability. I'd say 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem)\n3 solves implied problem...\nProposal directly addresses how to meet logging mandate. So 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Not specified