onship between R&D and compliance obligations fundamentally. So 2.\n\nEvidence sentence: It creates a new internal coordination format for logging duties but keeps compliance as a separate logging practice rather than weaving it into R&D.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nCreating part-time micro-team from existing staff for compliance. That's fairly common, mundane. Maybe interesting but common. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 interesting? Hmm. Many labs create cross-functional compliance teams. Probably common. So 1.\n\nEvidence: Part-time internal compliance micro-teams are a common organizational response to new regulatory mandates.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable\n1 = radically violates laws or sensibilities\n\nThis is acceptable. Common strategy, no violation. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Reassigning existing staff part-time to compliance duties respects labor norms and legal requirements.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nEasy to implement at low cost, no new headcount. So 4.\n\nEvidence: It uses existing headcount and requires only one day per week allocation and a weekly stand-up.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources.\n\nPro