g gaps is necessary but not sufficient; still need implement fixes. But rating says without regard for workability. If you could do it, would it solve? It identifies what to do, but doesn't do it. I'd say contributes.\n\nMaybe 4: reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. The audit yields prioritized fixes; closing gaps is implied. Text says \"Closing gaps before...\" Might be outcome.\n\nI'll pick effectiveness 3: reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Evidence sentence.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially/legal/politically acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Audit is common compliance practice. So 4.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Assign cross-functional team to catalogue... That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Maybe 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Audit of every high-risk system is significant effort. Could be 3 not 4. Low cost? Not low cost. Significant change? Maybe 3.\n\nLet's say implementability 3. Evidence: Requires dedicated engineers and compliance staff to inventory all systems and map logs, which is feasible but needs staffing time.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem. 4 = paradigm breaking... changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Audit is standard compliance. It's paradigm preserving? Definition 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. This is just audit. That's standard compliance. So paradigm_relatedness =1.\n\nOriginality: degree rare and ingenious. Common, mundane, boring =1. Gap audit is common. So 1.\n\nReturn format: echo id, then for ea