        "content": "{\n  \"ratings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2762\",\n      \"effectiveness_evidence\": \"The programme does not install or operate Article 12 logging for the lab’s own high-risk systems and therefore does not meet the mandate, but it reorients R&D to use mandated logs as a research input.\",\n      \"effectiveness\": 3,\n      \"applicability_evidence\": \"Using Article 12 lifetime logs as a data asset directly addresses the stated problem of adapting R&D to the logging mandate by turning compliance into a research programme.\",\n      \"applicability\": 4,\n      \"paradigm_relatedness_evidence\": \"It introduces a structured drift corpus and external deployer partnerships and changes the relationship between the lab’s R&D and its compliance obligations from cost to data source.\",\n      \"paradigm_relatedness\": 4,\n      \"originality_evidence\": \"Treating mandated event streams as a longitudinal model-behaviour corpus for distribution shift is unusual and shows imagination, though similar secondary-use of compliance data has been discussed.\",\n      \"originality\": 3,\n      \"acceptability_evidence\": \"Negotiating anonymised access to public-sector deployer logs for research is an uncommon strategy but does not violate legal or social norms when anonymisation is respected.\",\n      \"acceptability\": 3,\n      \"implementability_evidence\": \"Securing anonymised event-stream access from two public-sector deployers, obtaining data-sharing approvals and building statistical drift models requires significant partnership and legal effort.\",\n      \"implementability\": 2\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nWe need rate proposal P2762.\n\nText: Longitudinal Drift Corpus from Mandated Logs. Research programme treats Article 12's lifetime logging as data asset, building structured corpus of model-behaviour trajectories to study distribution shift... partner with two public-sector deployers already obligated under Article 72 to retain logs; negotiate anonymised access to their event streams and build statistical models... turns compliance cost into longitudinal evidence base... advancing publication record.\n\nNeed six subscales in order: effectiveness, applicability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab subject to regulation must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nDoes building corpus solve logging mandate? It uses logs but does not actually implement logging capabilities in own systems. The problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This proposal adapts R&D to use mandated logs as data asset. Does it solve meeting logging mandate? Probably contributes partially? It doesn't help lab meet its own Article 12 obligations. It leverages others' logs.\n\nEffectiveness definition: degree to which idea will solve problem. 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps, but only partial). 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. 1 = solves unrelated problem.\n\nProposal does not directly help lab implement logging for its own high-risk systems; it is a research program using others' logs. It adapts R&D but does not meet mandate. So maybe it contributes to adapt R&D accordingly? The stated problem includes \"how to meet the logging mandate and/or adapt its R&D accordingly\". So adapting R&D counts. Does building corpus help lab adapt R&D? Yes, reframes compliance as data asset.\n\nIs it reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? No, because meeting own logging mandate not addressed. Might contribute. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (it helps, but only partial). Might be applicability more relevant.\n\nLet's think: Effectiveness rating should be about solving problem of meeting logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. The proposal adapts R&D, turning compliance cost into research. That is a response to mandate. It does not help meet logging mandate for own systems. So partial solution. Probably 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y, which applies to stated problem)\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\nProposal: treats Article 12 lifetime logging as data asset. The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This directly relates: using mandated logs for research is adapting R&D accordingly. So solves identified problem that is directly related? The identified problem is meeting mandate; adapting R&D is part of /or. Might be 4.\n\nBut applicability maybe 3 because it doesn't address own logging mandate, only uses others' logs. Hmm.\n\nLet's interpret: Applicability is degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. It clearly applies: building corpus from mandated logs is a response to mandate. Could be 4 = solves identified problem directly related. I'd say 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance\n\nProposal treats logging as data asset, partners with deployers, turns compliance cost into evidence base. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations: compliance becomes source of research data. That's paradigm stretching? It changes relationship. Maybe 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science, new partners). Could be 4 if it addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It advances publication record on model reliability. Might be breaking? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Hmm.\n\nThe definition 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, OR addresses larger problem of what the lab researches. It does both? Introduces corpus, changes relationship. Might be 4. Let's pick 3 to be safe? Paradigm stretching vs breaking nuance.\n\nIt treats mandated logs as data asset - that's new element and changes relationship. That seems paradigm breaking? The instrument: paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This does change relationship and introduce new elements. Might be 3? Stretching is changes that relationship. Breaking is introduces new elements AND changes relationship. So this has both. So 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious, imaginative, surprising\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common\n\nUsing compliance logs for research on drift is unusual but plausible. Is it rare? Maybe 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Might be 4? Not expressed before? Probably similar ideas exist. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 = radically violates laws\n\nPartnering with public-sector deployers for anonymised access to event logs. Needs data sharing, privacy concerns. Acceptable? Probably acceptable if anonymised. Somewhat uncommon. Might be 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. Could be 4 common? Not common. So 3.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nNegotiate anonymised access to event streams from two public-sector deployers, build statistical models. That's significant change, requires partnerships, data agreements, legal review. Not easy low cost. Reasonably feasible? Might be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Could be 2 = significant change, expensive or difficult. Negotiating access to logs of high-risk AI systems is difficult due to privacy, commercial sensitivity. Might be 2.\n\nThe proposal commits scarce implementation resources. It requires partnership negotiations. Likely significant change. I'd give 2.\n\nLet's decide final scores:\neffectiveness: 3\napplicability: 4? Might be 3. Hmm.\n\nCheck effectiveness definition again: degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. The proposal does not help meet own logging mandate, only uses others' logs for research. Does that count as adapting R&D accordingly? Yes. So it partially solves. 3.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. It applies directly to adapting R&D to mandate. I'd say 4 = solves identified problem directly related.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4\n\nOriginality: 3\n\nAcceptability: 3\n\nImplementability: 2? Could be 3. Let's pick 2 = significant change, expensive/difficult but not impossible. Negotiating access to logs from public deployers is difficult. I'll go with 2.\n\nProvide output format: echo id, then each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed first write one sentence of evidence for that subscale (reasoning before number). Then give its score as integer.\n\nProbably format like:\nP2762\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nEnsure order listed: effectiveness, applicability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide.\n\nLet's produce final answer.\n\n",
