ant change, or expensive/difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nEncode schema assertions in pytest, wire into pre-merge webhook, calibrate over three sprints on pilot. That's reasonably feasible, some changes. Might be easy? Requires engineering effort. Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = ...\n\nProblem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal directly addresses meeting logging mandate by ensuring minimum fields present. So applicability 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nWill gate ensure logs present? It ensures schema present at merge, but does it guarantee logs actually recorded over lifetime? Might ensure traceability wired in from day one, kills add logging later. Probably will solve main problem of meeting Article 12 logging mandate for prototypes. Could be partial: doesn't guarantee runtime logging quality. But reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Actually might fully solve compliance for development stage.\n\nThe stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Gate ensures no prototype ships without traceability wired in. That seems to solve.\n\nI'd give effectiveness = 3? Let's see definition: 4 = reasonable and will solve the stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Does gate solve logging mandate? It ensures schemas exist, not necessarily logs recorded correctly in production. Might still need runtime recording. Could be partial.\n\nI'll rate effectiveness 3 (contribute to solution, partial).\n\nNow produce output with evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Return rating echoing id exactly. Provide lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid P2954\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ... then paradigm_relatedness: 2 etc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The gate adds a CI tool and schema assertions for logging but still treats compliance as a logging practice rather than redefining the lab’s R&D mission.\nScore 2\n\noriginality_evidence: Automated merge-blocking tests for regulatory log schemas are a recognizable shift-left practice and not particularly ingenious or surprising in an R&D lab.\nScore 2? maybe 3. Hmm.\n\nLet's pick originality 3? \"unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\". Might be unusual for AI labs? Could be 3.\n\nI think originality = 3 is plausible.\n\nLet's decide: I'd give 2 for originality (interesting). Keep consistent.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Failing a merge for missing compliance metadata is a normal engineering quality gate and violates no legal, social or business norms.\nScore 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Encoding four schema assertions in pytest and wiring a pre-merge webhook is a feasible engineering change requiring a pilot calibration over three sprints.\nScore 3\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly creates the Article 12(3) minimum log fields at prototype merge, which is exactly the logging mandate the lab must meet.\nScore 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If implemented, the gate would prevent prototypes lacking traceability from shipping, materially contributing to compliance though it does not guarantee correct runtime logging throughout system lifetime.\nScore 3\n\nReturn.\n\n",
