t expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nLog-integrity gate in CI for regulatory compliance? Might be somewhat uncommon but not surprising. Probably 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Or 2 = interesting.\n\nReference class: responses an experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Many might suggest logging library or manual audit. CI gate is somewhat novel. I'd say originality 3.\n\nEvidence: Using merge-blocking contract tests for Article 12 log schema is unusual among typical compliance checklists.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nGate embeds compliance verification into daily dev loop. That's weaving logging into science? It changes relationship? Maybe slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. The description says embedding compliance verification into daily dev loop catches gaps... That's weaving logging into R&D process. Could be paradigm stretching = 3.\n\nParadigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). Logging woven into science fits. So 3.\n\nLet's output JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\".\n\nProbably format:\n\nP2700\napplicability_evide