gree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? Do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Doing analytics on logs to produce research output - is that part of stated problem? Stated problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This does adapt R&D using logs, so applies. Probably 4 = solves identified problem directly related.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\nLevels: 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving\n\nProposal treats logs as primary scientific data, converts regulatory obligation into unique dataset, positions lab as reference point. That's changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations - logging woven into science. Might be paradigm stretching (3) or breaking (4)? Does it address larger problem of what lab researches? It produces generalisable findings on temporal model degradation... That is still within AI reliability research, maybe expands but not change what lab researches fundamentally? It says converts regulatory obligation into unique dataset no competitor has access to, positioning lab as reference point for empirical AI-lifetime-stability research. That's changing relationship. Paradigm breaking requires introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Might be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). I'd say 3.\n\nOriginality: degree rare and ingenious. Not expressed before AND ingenious... 4. Unusual interesting shows some imagination 3. Interesting 2. Common mundane 1.\n\nIs treating mandatory logs as scientific data for drift analytics common? Possibly unusual but not extremely rare. Many labs do post-hoc analysis of logs. But specific to EU AI Act Article12 biometric systems and building research workstream... Might be unusual, interesting; shows some imagination = 3. Could be 2? Probably 3.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Easy low cost or non-radical changes =4. Some changes reasonably feasible =3. Significant change expensive difficult but not impossible =2. Totally infeasible =1.\n\nAssign two postdocs to build lightweight analytics layer atop existing logging pipeline already being retrofitted for compliance; define taxonomy first quarter then quarterly analyses. That sounds reasonably feasible, some changes. Might be 3. Not easy low cost? Two postdocs is resource but maybe feasible. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms =4. Somewhat uncommon unusual strategies do not offend sensibilities =3. Offends somewhat =2. Radically violates =1.\n\nPublishing results on drift using logs from biometric systems - may involve privacy concerns? Logs include input data for which search led to match, identification of natural persons... Article 12(3)(c)(d). Using those for research could be sensitive. But proposal says use logs as primary scientific data. Might be acceptable with anonymization? Could offend sensibilities somewhat? Possibly still acceptable if compliant with GDPR. Probably 3? Or 4 common strategies violate no norms? Using compliance data for internal research is maybe uncommon but not offensive. I'd say 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities.\n\nAlternatively acceptability 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Might be acceptable.\n\nLet's decide: effectiveness evidence sentence then score 3. applicability evidence sentence then score 4. paradigm_relatedness evidence sentence then score 3. originality evidence sentence then score 3. implementability evidence sentence then score 3. acceptability evidence sentence then score 3? Or 4?\n\nThe proposal uses biometric system logs containing personal data; publishing findings may raise privacy concerns. Might be somewhat uncommon. I'll give acceptability 3.\n\nProvide output echo id P2416.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instructions say return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Probably plain lines.\n\nLet's output structured:\n\nid: P2416\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure sentences end with period.\n\nLet's craft evidence sentences.\n\nEffectiveness: The workstream does not create the mandatory logs but assumes they are being retrofitted for compliance and then extracts scientific value from them, contributing to adaptation rather than guaranteeing traceability.\n\nApplicability: The proposal directly addresses how the lab adapts its R&D to the Article 12 logging mandate by repurposing required lifetime event logs as research data.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: It changes the relationship between compliance and science by weaving mandatory logging into an ongoing reliability research program rather than treating logging as separate overhead.\n\nOriginality: Repurposing legally mandated biometric logs for longitudinal drift analytics is unusual for an R&D lab and shows imagination in creating a proprietary empirical dataset.\n\nImplementability: Building a lightweight analytics layer atop an already planned logging pipeline with two postdocs and quarterly analyses is reasonably feasible with modest resource change.\n\nAcceptability: Using compliance logs for internal research and public publication is uncommon but does not violate legal nor