r modifies paradigm.\nIt introduces new elements tools processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's level 2. Could be 2. Not breaking relationship between R&D and compliance. It's still compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies violate no norms. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Yes. Acceptability=4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nSome changes, reasonably feasible measures. Two-week sprint refactor five active architectures, codify ADR. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Level 3. Not easy low cost? Maybe 3. Could be 3.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale_evidence sentence then subscale integer.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order list <subscale>_evidence first then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? Probably plain text. Safer to give structured.\n\nWill output:\n\nid: P1108\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft concise one sentence.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly builds logging capabilities required by Article 12(1)-(3) into system design for high-risk AI systems. applicability: 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Embedding log schema, retention, tamper-evidence and Article 12(2)(a)-(c) events at design stage would ensure automatic event recording mandated for the lab's R&D. effectiveness: 4\n\noriginality_evidence: Shift-left logging and architecture decision records are common software engineering practices for compliance features. originality: 1\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The idea adds logging templates and review steps but keeps compliance as a logging practice rather than changing how R&D relates to compliance obligations. paradigm_relatedness: 2\n\nacceptability_evidence: Redesi