o research advances R&D adaptation. Might solve both? It does not implement logging itself. So maybe contributes.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? Do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. The proposal builds observatory from lifetime logs. Does that directly address meeting logging mandate? It uses logs, but primary outcome is research dataset. That's adaptation of R&D accordingly. So applicable. Probably 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Or 3 = solves implied problem. I think applicability is 4: it applies to adapting R&D using mandated logs.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented. Build internal analytics platform, assign two postdocs, ingest logs from three pipelines rolling 90-day window, running periodic statistical tests. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Two postdocs is resource intensive. Might be some changes or reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Using anonymized corpus for studying performance degradation. Logging data includes personal data? Annex III involves biometric identification: recording period of each use, reference database, input data for which search led to match, identification of natural persons involved in verification. That's sensitive personal data. Anonymizing may be difficult/legal risk. But proposal says curated anonymized corpus. Might be somewhat uncommon but does not offend sensibilities. Acceptability 3? Or 4 common strategies violate no norms? Using logs for internal research may raise privacy concerns. Could be somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities => 3.\n\nWe need output format: echo id, then for each subscale in order give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON-like? Instruction says give \"<subscal