retching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is standing internal function owning documentation, quarterly audits, gate. That's standard compliance and logging practice; lab way unchanged. So 1? Or 2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. It introduces governance office, which is a process. Might be 2.\n\nThe description: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice =2. That fits.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nRecord-lifecycle governance office is common compliance practice. So originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nThus ratings:\napplicability 3\neffectiveness 3\nimplementability 3\nacceptability 4\nparadigm_relatedness 2? maybe 1. Let's decide 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. The proposal is essentially a documentation owner team - very standard. I'd say 1.\n\nCheck anchor: paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; the lab's way of working is unchanged. Yes.\n\nOriginality 1.\n\nLet's produce output with subscale_evidence sentences then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale (applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality), in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably JSON? Return rating echoing id. Probably simple lines.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2736\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 3\n...\n\nLet's ensure one sentence evidence each.\n\nMake sure sentences end wi