estone. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Two weeks of senior staff is cost. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms. This is standard. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. Does risk-tier classification solve logging mandate? It helps prioritize resources but does not itself implement logging. It contributes to solution, partial. So 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial solution). Could be 4? It would solve problem without regard for workability? No, it doesn't provide logs. It prevents over-investment. It's partial. So 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves an identified problem directly related to stated problem? The stated problem is decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Classification addresses adaptation of R&D resource allocation to mandate. That's directly related. Do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. It identifies which projects need full Article 12 obligations. That is directly related. So 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem.\n\nNeed format: return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence first then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating... For each subscale... give \"..._evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" . Might be plain text. Safer to output structured lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1940\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence