t, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nResearch program developing statistical and ML methods, synthetic log generators, benchmark... That's a research program requiring scarce implementation resources. That's significant change / expensive. Might be 2? \"significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\". Could be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Research program is maybe expensive. Given approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. This suggests it's a research bet. Probably implementability = 2 (significant change, expensive/difficult). Or 3? I'd say 2.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nDeveloping drift detection on logs, using anonymised partner logs. Acceptable. Uncommon? Maybe 3? It's research. Probably acceptable. Common strategies? Not common but does not offend. So acceptability = 3? Could be 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Research programs are common. Might be 4.\n\nLet's choose 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Developing ML methods is standard. I'd go with 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal develops methods to use mandated logs for safety science. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations: using compliance data as research input. That's paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements and changes relationship. Might be 3 (changes that relationship). Paradigm breaking requires introduces new elements AND changes relationship OR addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This does address larger problem? Maybe not break paradigm. Could be 3.\n\nOriginality:\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious/imaginative/surprising\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nLong-horizon drift detection in operational AI logs is unusual but not necessarily ingenious? It's interesting. Could be 3. Maybe 2? I'd say unusual, interesting; shows some imagination => 3.\n\nNow produce output with subscale_evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale (applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality), in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give ... Might be plain text.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2235\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 3\n...\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nEnsure one sentence.\n\nLet's craft sentences:\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal uses Article 12 mandated lifetime logs as input for research, which directly adapts the lab’s R&D to the logging mandate rather than implementing the logs themselves. That's one sentence.\n\nScore 3.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Developing drift detection will not create the required logging capability but will make the mandated logs scientifically useful, partially addressing the adapt-R&D aspect of the problem. Score 3.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building synthetic multi-year log generators and benchmarking change-point algori