        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need output rating for P1074.\n\nWe need six subscales in order: acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, applicability, effectiveness\n\nEach with evidence sentence then score.\n\nWe must apply anchored levels exactly.\n\nProposal: Centralised Log-Retention Service on Shared HPC Cluster... ingests retains indexes event logs... dedicated partition on current Lustre-backed storage array with lifecycle policies matching Article 12... lightweight query API so post-market monitoring... Reusing existing infrastructure keeps capital expenditure minimal...\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but is not totally unacceptable\n1 = radically violates laws or sensibilities\n\nCentralised logging service is common compliance practice, no violation. Likely 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nEvidence: It reuses existing HPC infrastructure for compliant log retention and audit access, which aligns with standard regulatory practice.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nIt uses existing cluster, dedicated partition, lifecycle policies, lightweight query API. Sounds easy to implement at low cost. Probably 4? Might be 3 = some changes reasonably feasible. \"Provision a dedicated partition... plus lightweight query API\". That's non-radical changes. I'd say 4 = easy to implement at low cost.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D