res: paradigm_relatedness 3, originality 3, acceptability 4, implementability 3, applicability 4, effectiveness 3.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. Provide evidence then score. Likely list.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid: P1659\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\netc.\n\nMake one sentence evidence each.\n\nEnsure one sentence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal reframes compliance logging as a public product that creates market differentiation and trusted authority, changing the relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. Score 3.\n\nOriginality evidence: Publishing an open-source reference implementation tailored to Annex III point 1(a) logging fields is unusual for an R&D lab and shows imagination beyond internal tooling. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Open-sourcing a compliance reference codebase does not violate legal, social or business norms and is publicly defensible. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building and maintaining the repository requires a small dedicated team for eighteen months, which is reasonably feasible though not trivially cheap. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The reference codebase directly demonstrates how to meet Article 12 logging requirements for biometric identity verification systems. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, the reference architecture would provide a compliant logging design that can be adopted, contributing substantially but not automatically completing internal system integration. Score 3.\n\nReturn.",
