dentifying which... still lacks. That suggests not complete solution. Maybe effectiveness 3 = contributes.\n\nLet's pick 3? Hmm \"will solve the stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\". If implemented fully, it would meet logging mandate. The proposal includes mapping and instrumentation. Might be enough. I'll give 4? Safer to say reasonable and will contribute, not guarantee full compliance for all systems. I think 3.\n\nImplementability (1-4): easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes.\nSix-week sprint, no new hardware, uses existing Kubernetes clusters, modest upgrade. That's easy to implement at low cost? Some changes, reasonably feasible. Maybe 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. It is retrofit, engineering sprint. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms. Logging compliance is standard. So 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nAnchored: 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal retrofits pipelines with log schemas, absorbs compliance into opex. Introduces new elements tools? Still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces structured log emitters but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4