g, transparency but doesn't provide logs. So contributes partially. Maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but partial). Could be 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. I think it contributes to defensibility but not core logging. I'd rate 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nPublishing technical dossier, open source schema. Common strategies that violate no norms? It's somewhat uncommon but does not offend sensibilities. Could be 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Or 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. Publishing compliance details might be sensitive IP. Could be acceptable? Probably acceptable, not offending. I'd say 3 = somewhat uncommon.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nCompliance office collaborate with lead engineers to author dossier over one quarter, mapping each design decision. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. That's 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Might need engineer time. Could be 3.\n\nThus scores: originality 3, paradigm_relatedness 3, applicability 3, effectiveness 3, acceptability 3, implementability 3.\n\nMaybe originality 2? Let's see anchored: 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Publishing whitepaper is unusual but not ingenious. Might be 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. The proposal still treats compliance as logging practice (documentation). It adds public disclosure. Could be 2. Hmm.\n\nThe definition: 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born fro